# 32 CFR Part 117 — National Industrial Security Program Operating Manual (NISPOM), contractor obligations.
# One attested row per paragraph of §§117.7–117.23 that places an obligation on the contractor (the entity, its
# SMO, FSO, ITPSO, ISSM, KMP, governing board, couriers, employees and consultants as the rule addresses them).
#
# Source of truth: U.S. Government Publishing Office, Code of Federal Regulations annual edition, bulk XML
#   https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/CFR/2025/title-32/CFR-2025-title32-vol1.xml
#   sha256 f87c04b1789f61c69d04555d62f6835251998ce3eefcb39e99e9c682ab4a8059, Title 32 revised as of July 1, 2025
#   (the 2026 Title 32 edition is not yet published), retrieved 2026-10-14. Public domain (17 U.S.C. 105).
#   Part history as printed: [85 FR 83312, Dec. 21, 2020, as amended at 86 FR 46598, Aug. 19, 2021]. eCFR versioner
#   (api/versioner/v1/versions/title-32.json?part=117, retrieved 2026-10-14) lists no amendment after 2021-08-19.
# Every id, text, stem and item below is copied verbatim from that XML by a generator; nothing is paraphrased.
# Ids are the CFR paragraph citations as printed (117.8(c)(1)); italic fifth/sixth-level designators are written
# without italics (117.11(d)(2)(i)(A)(1)). Because ids contain parentheses the rows are an array (`_rows`) and the
# keyed map `obligations` is built from it.
#
# How each designated paragraph was classified (a generator first pass, then a paragraph-by-paragraph review):
#   row        a contractor obligation (will / must / shall / is required to / may not / cannot / will not)
#   lead-in    "The contractor will:" paragraphs whose sub-paragraphs are separate obligations; not a row, its
#              text is carried verbatim as `stem` on each sub-paragraph row
#   folded     a list fragment with no verb of its own ("(i) Death.", "(ii) Change in name.") or the content list
#              of a report, briefing, plan or request; carried verbatim in `items` of its nearest row ancestor
#   gov        a duty of the CSA, GCA, USG, DoD, DHS, DGR, COR, OCA or investigative provider (out of scope)
#   not-a-row  a heading, scope or definitional statement, permission ("may"), or a cross-reference only
#
