#       (e)(5), (e)(6)(v), (e)(7), (e)(7)(i), (e)(7)(iii), (e)(8), (e)(10), (e)(10)(i), (e)(12)
#
# Sections not covered: §§117.1–117.6 (purpose, applicability, definitions, policy, information collections,
# responsibilities of USD(I&S), DCSA, DOE, NRC, DNI, DHS, CSA heads, component heads and ISOO: no contractor
# duties) and §117.24 (cognizant security office directory).
#
# Judgement calls:
#   - Conditional obligations ("If the contractor ...", "When requested by the CSA ...") are rows: the attestation
#     is true when the obligation is met or its stated trigger has not occurred. Whole programmes that apply only
#     to some contractors are gated by declared flags instead (see `_gates`).
#   - §117.7(c), (c)(2) (KMP list; governing-board exclusion action), §117.9(g) (temporary-exclusion conditions),
#     §117.9(n), (o) (return or disposal on termination; no bids while invalidated), §117.10(a)(1)(iii), (f)(1),
#     (j)(1), (k)(2) (conditions on granting access) are rows although the paragraph is framed as a permission or a
#     CSA action, because each sets a condition the contractor must meet.
#   - §117.8(f)(1)(iii) ("Reports involving classified foreign government information will be reported to the
#     Director, DTSA") is read as the CDC's duty; §117.8(f)(1)(ii) (handling of CDC-reported information) as DoD's.
#   - §117.9(c)(1)–(9) (eligibility requirements: "the entity must ...") are rows with the lead-in as stem.
#   - §117.13(d)(5)(i), §117.15(h)(1), (h)(2)(i)(A), (h)(3)(i)(A), §117.17(a)(1)(ii)(A), §117.19(e)(3)(ii)(B)(2) are
#     permissions ("may retain", "are authorized to disclose") and are not rows.
#   - §117.15(d)(6)(i) and its conditions (CSA-approved alternative alarm systems) are a permission path, not rows;
#     the proposal contents in §117.15(d)(6)(ii) are rows because the proposal "must include" them.
#   - §117.19(c)(3)–(6) and (c)(13) only point to §117.14(l)–(o) and §117.17; they are not rows (the obligations are
#     counted once, at §117.14 and §117.17). §117.23(e)(9) repeats §117.14(q) in full and is kept as a row (both
#     gated by handles_restricted_data).
#   - §117.16(b)(4) carries an obligation of its own and a list; it and its sub-paragraphs are all rows.
#   - §117.22(b)(2) is the only contractor duty in §117.22 ("Participating entities will cooperate with DHS
#     security officials").
#
# Source irregularities, kept as printed: §117.9(h) places (i) and (ii) directly under a letter (no numeric
# level), cited here as 117.9(h)(i), 117.9(h)(ii) (both out of scope); §117.3(b) definitions repeat (1)–(5)
# under several defined terms (out of scope); §117.19(e)(1) and (f)(1) and §117.19(d)(2)(i), (e)(2)(i) chain three
# designators in one printed paragraph (each designator is its own row id here).
# Could not read: nothing; every in-scope paragraph parsed with a valid, unique citation.
# Not carried (tables and figures, printed as GPOTABLE in the XML): Table 1 to §117.7(c)(2) (exclusion resolution
# language, referenced by row 117.7(c)(2) "see table"), Table 2 to §117.7(o), Table 1 to §117.9(g)(4), Figure 1 to
# §117.13(c)(1), Tables 1–5 to §117.19 (g)(2), (g)(3), (i)(3), (i)(4), Table 1 to §117.23(e)(6)(ii).
# `stem` joins nested lead-ins with " … " (5 rows: 117.8(a)(2)(i)–(iii) and two in §117.15); the separator is
# not source text, each side of it is verbatim.
#
# Input: input.nispom.obligations["117.8(c)(1)"] == true means the contractor's evidence shows that obligation
# met. Each gate flag in `_gates` must be declared true or false at input.nispom.<flag>. Fail-closed: a missing
# or non-object `obligations` container is a FAIL-CLOSED violation; a missing, false or non-true value for an
# applicable row, an undeclared flag and an unknown id are violations. Rollup per section.
#
# Query: POST /v1/data/nispom/main/compliance_report

